{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-72252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13971771/assets/28079ead5ebb4649900653172d11c25f3ff489c6b34772094a1ba947c18ef3e1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13971771/state.json" "file_sha256" "56c9c4ff7b77ec9318cfb3a0178718f81d02e6519cc20dfa50a310154aba2afc"} {"question" "q-72252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13971771/state.json" "file_sha256" "56c9c4ff7b77ec9318cfb3a0178718f81d02e6519cc20dfa50a310154aba2afc"} {"question" "q-72252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13971771/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "137eb9aea9d33d7ab5b15f390222fa52430076aa2c0d657a7dd6401ae8ed8f74"} {"question" "q-72252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13971771/state.jso